      mechanism: "Chylomicron and VLDL triglyceride is hydrolyzed by endothelial lipoprotein lipase, releasing fatty acids to muscle and adipose tissue. Hepatic lipase and remnant processing progressively shrink particles. LDL binds hepatic LDL receptors through ApoB-100, enters clathrin-coated endosomes, releases from the receptor at low pH, and is delivered to lysosomes while the receptor normally recycles. Intracellular cholesterol suppresses HMG-CoA reductase and LDL-receptor transcription through SREBP signaling. Statins lower hepatic cholesterol and activate SREBP2-driven receptor production; ezetimibe reduces intestinal and biliary cholesterol entry through NPC1L1; PCSK9 antibodies protect surface LDL receptors from lysosomal destruction; inclisiran reduces hepatic PCSK9 synthesis. When triglycerides are high, cholesterol may be distributed across many smaller particles, so LDL-C can appear acceptable while ApoB or non-HDL-C reveals persistent particle burden. Lipoprotein(a) adds an inherited Apo(a)-bearing LDL-like particle with proatherogenic and prothrombotic properties that ordinary lifestyle change affects little.",
