      description: "The 2026 multisociety dyslipidemia guideline is a risk-and-goal framework that directs clinicians to lower cumulative atherogenic ApoB and LDL-particle exposure with lifestyle, statins, and selected nonstatins to prevent ASCVD events. It restores explicit LDL-C and non-HDL-C goals while keeping statins as the pharmacologic foundation. Risk is not inferred from LDL alone: established ASCVD, prior events, diabetes, CKD, familial hypercholesterolemia, coronary calcium, age, blood pressure, smoking, and risk-enhancing conditions change how aggressively exposure should be lowered. PREVENT-ASCVD equations estimate 10- and 30-year risk for adults without known ASCVD. In secondary prevention, very-high-risk patients generally target LDL-C below 55 mg/dL and non-HDL-C below 85 mg/dL; other secondary-prevention patients generally target LDL-C below 70 mg/dL. The reason for lower goals at higher risk is absolute benefit: the same relative LDL reduction prevents more events when baseline event probability is greater. Goals guide escalation, but treatment still begins with shared decisions, maximally tolerated therapy, adherence, and correction of secondary causes.",
      mechanism: "Atherosclerotic risk reflects cumulative ApoB-particle exposure across time. Lowering LDL earlier reduces the area under that lifetime exposure curve, while lowering it after an event reduces ongoing plaque growth and recurrent events. Statins increase LDL-receptor expression and have the strongest foundational outcomes evidence. If the expected reduction or goal is not reached, adding a drug at a complementary step can produce greater receptor-mediated clearance without forcing one agent beyond its safe range. Ezetimibe limits NPC1L1-mediated cholesterol absorption; bempedoic acid inhibits ATP-citrate lyase upstream of HMG-CoA reductase; PCSK9 antibodies preserve LDL receptors. Lipoprotein(a) is measured at least once because it is largely inherited and may explain residual risk; selective ApoB testing helps when LDL-C underestimates particle number. Coronary calcium can reclassify uncertain primary-prevention decisions because it directly demonstrates calcified plaque burden.",
