      mechanism: "HMG-CoA reductase normally commits acetyl-CoA-derived substrate to the mevalonate pathway. Reversible statin binding lowers intracellular cholesterol, causing SREBP2 to move from endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi for activation and then to the nucleus, where it increases LDL-receptor transcription. More receptors capture LDL and remnants from plasma. Reduced mevalonate also changes isoprenoid intermediates and vascular signaling, but event prevention tracks strongly with the magnitude and duration of ApoB-particle lowering. The same pathway explains several cautions: skeletal muscle exposure can impair energy and membrane processes; strong metabolism or transporter inhibition can raise statin concentration; and pregnancy usually removes the preventive indication because short interruption has little effect on lifetime risk while fetal cholesterol synthesis is biologically important. Individual statins differ in prodrug status, half-life, lipophilicity, CYP metabolism, transporters, renal elimination, and dose ceiling, so class membership does not make them interchangeable milligram for milligram.",
