      description: "Hepatic cholesterol synthesis is greatest overnight, so short-acting statins are generally placed in the evening to align peak inhibition with peak pathway activity. Simvastatin, immediate-release fluvastatin, pravastatin, and immediate-release lovastatin fit that logic. Atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, and pitavastatin have longer effective activity and can usually be taken at any consistent time. Formulation can override the class rule: immediate-release lovastatin is taken with the evening meal because food increases absorption, whereas lovastatin extended release is taken at bedtime without food; fluvastatin extended release can be taken once daily at any time and must not be crushed. Timing is therefore a pharmacokinetic decision, not a ritual. Adherence at a consistent feasible time often matters more than moving a long-acting statin by a few hours.",
