      description: "Statin-associated muscle symptoms are a spectrum, not one diagnosis. Symmetric aches, cramps, heaviness, or fatigue can occur with normal CK and may or may not be caused by the statin. Toxic myopathy produces objective weakness and greater CK elevation; rhabdomyolysis is extensive myocyte breakdown that releases myoglobin, potassium, phosphate, CK, and purines, thereby risking acute kidney injury and arrhythmia. Myoglobin can obstruct and oxidatively injure renal tubules, which explains why hydration, urine output, creatinine, and potassium matter urgently. The rare anti-HMGCR immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy causes progressive proximal weakness and markedly elevated CK that persists or worsens after the statin is stopped because antibodies sustain muscle injury independently of circulating drug. That persistence is therefore the diagnostic clue. Most patients with ordinary symptoms can ultimately tolerate a lower dose, another statin, intermittent dosing, or combination therapy, so one episode should not automatically end all LDL-lowering treatment.",
