      description: "Alirocumab, branded Praluent, is a subcutaneous fully human monoclonal antibody that binds circulating PCSK9 and prevents PCSK9-mediated destruction of hepatic LDL receptors. More receptors therefore recycle to the cell surface and remove LDL particles, producing a large additional LDL reduction. It has cardiovascular outcomes evidence and a current U.S. indication for reducing major adverse cardiovascular events in adults at increased risk because sustained particle clearance reduces plaque-related events. Common starting regimens are 75 mg every two weeks or 300 mg every four weeks, with adjustment based on LDL response and the exact indication. It is not the same molecule or device as evolocumab and is not the same platform as inclisiran, so schedules and outcome claims cannot simply be copied. Minimal CYP metabolism means few classic drug interactions, but a dose can still fail through wrong storage, incomplete injection, missed administration, or a receptor-negative HoFH phenotype.",
      mechanism: "Alirocumab binds free PCSK9, thereby preventing its interaction with the LDL receptor. Without PCSK9, endosomal LDL dissociates and the receptor returns to the surface instead of being delivered to lysosomes. Repeated receptor use increases clearance of LDL and other ApoB particles, which explains why the laboratory effect is much larger than blocking one round of LDL uptake. Antibody proteolysis, not hepatic CYP or renal filtration, clears the drug, so classic CYP interactions are not expected. LDL can vary near the end of a four-week interval in some patients because antibody concentration falls, so dose adjustment is based on measured response and current label instructions. Functional receptor availability remains necessary; a patient with no LDL-receptor activity will have a limited pathway response and may need receptor-independent HoFH treatment because there is no receptor for the antibody to preserve.",
