      aliases: ["Vascepa", "icosapent", "icosapent ethel", "pure EPA prescription", "EPA ethyl ester", "prescription EPA"],
      brandExamples: ["Vascepa"],
      class: "Purified eicosapentaenoic-acid ethyl ester; prescription triglyceride and cardiovascular-risk therapy",
      classPathway: ["Hydrolysis to EPA", "Reduced hepatic triglyceride synthesis and VLDL secretion", "EPA-specific membrane, oxidation, inflammation, and plaque effects"],
      usedToTreat: "With maximally tolerated statin therapy, reduction of cardiovascular events in selected high-risk adults with triglycerides at least 150 mg/dL and established cardiovascular disease or diabetes plus risk factors; also adjunctive triglyceride lowering in adults with severe hypertriglyceridemia at least 500 mg/dL.",
      description: "Icosapent ethyl, branded Vascepa, is a prescription purified EPA ethyl ester that lowers triglycerides by reducing hepatic triglyceride synthesis and VLDL secretion and increasing triglyceride-rich-particle clearance; in qualifying statin-treated high-risk adults, it also reduces selected cardiovascular events. It is not generic fish oil or interchangeable with mixed EPA-DHA products. The standard total dose is 4 g/day as 2 g twice daily with food. It is also labeled for severe triglyceride lowering, but the effect on preventing pancreatitis in that population has not been established. EPA can increase atrial fibrillation or flutter requiring hospitalization and can increase bleeding, especially with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy. Capsules are swallowed whole; purity and dose are central to why supplement substitution is inappropriate.",
      mechanism: "Intestinal lipases hydrolyze the ethyl ester and release EPA for absorption, transport, and incorporation into phospholipids. EPA reduces hepatic diacylglycerol acyltransferase activity, triglyceride synthesis, and ApoB-VLDL assembly; increases beta oxidation and lipoprotein-lipase-mediated clearance; and changes membrane composition, eicosanoids, oxidation, inflammation, endothelial signaling, and plaque biology. No single pathway fully explains the cardiovascular outcome. Unlike DHA-containing products, purified EPA generally does not produce the same LDL-C rise. Effects on atrial electrophysiology and platelet or eicosanoid signaling plausibly explain AF and bleeding signals. Food improves administration consistency and capsule swallowing but does not turn lower-dose OTC oil into equivalent therapy.",
