      mechanism: "MTP transfers triglyceride, cholesterol ester, and phospholipid to nascent ApoB in the endoplasmic reticulum. In enterocytes this creates chylomicrons; in hepatocytes it creates VLDL. Lomitapide binds and inhibits MTP, so ApoB cannot acquire enough lipid, is targeted for proteasomal degradation, and fewer particles are secreted. Less VLDL means less IDL and LDL downstream, bypassing the need for LDL-receptor clearance. Triglyceride that cannot leave accumulates in liver, directly explaining steatosis and possible steatohepatitis or cirrhosis. Reduced chylomicron formation leaves dietary fat in the gut, producing dose- and fat-dependent GI symptoms and reducing vitamins A, D, E, K and essential fatty acids. CYP3A4 extensively metabolizes lomitapide, so inhibitors can sharply increase exposure and liver toxicity; the drug also increases simvastatin, lovastatin, and warfarin exposure.",
